<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amol</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amol is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> province, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on the Haraz river near the Caspian coast. In the early Islamic centuries it was the chief city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, ruled in turn by local dynasties such as the Bavandids, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a> and the Marashis. The historian al-Tabari was born there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amol.jpg" alt="Amol" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ENMIRAN · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Two_Historical_Bridges_of_Amol_at_Night.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Amol is a city in the Central District of Amol County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and the capital of both the county and the district. It stands on the Haraz River, less than 20 kilometres south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and less than 10 kilometres north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> mountains, at an altitude of 76 metres. It lies 124 kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> by one measure and 60 kilometres west of the provincial capital, Sari. Mount Damavand, the highest peak in Iran and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, rises to the south. The Haraz route is one of the oldest roads joining the north of Iran to the central plateau. Most Amoli people speak the Mazandarani language as their mother tongue, and Persian is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Amol is one of the oldest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its foundation goes back to the Amardi tribe, who inhabited the region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and are mentioned by the Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>. A number of historians and geographers believe it was established in the 1st millennium BCE, and some attribute its birth to the reign of the mythological king Tahmuras. Excavation at Qaleh Kesh has found ancient jewellery dated to the 1st millennium BCE or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>.</p><p>In the era of the Alid dynasties and the Marashi dynasty, Amol was the capital of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and it was also the capital of the Bavand and Ziyarid dynasties. Its inhabitants embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> during the reign of the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> (775 to 785), after initially resisting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. In the ninth century it was one of the largest cities in Iran. During the Umayyad period Sa'id ibn al-As led an army of 4,000 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> in the reign of Muawiyah I, and his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhan-the-great">Farrukhan the Great</a> lasted two years. The city has suffered earthquake and flood damage several times and recovered each time. The historian Muhammad ibn Jarir al-Tabari, who lived from 839 to 923, came from Amol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Amol is known as a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation. The Rice Research Institute of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> was established there in 1963. The old bazaar, whose origins date to before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, forms the core of the old city. Among its historic sites are the Mausoleum of Mir Bozorg, the Jameh Mosque and Malek Bahman Castle. The 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 237,528 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1349 he murdered Hasan and took control of <b>Amol</b> and much of Mazandaran.</blockquote><a href="/state/afrasiyabids-of-mazandaran">Afrasiyabids of Mazandaran</a></li><li><blockquote>With Daylamite support he took <b>Amol</b> and established a Zaydi imamate, calling himself al-Da'i ila'l-Haqq, 'the summoner to the truth'.</blockquote><a href="/state/alavid-dynasty">Alavid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty ruled in three main branches: the Kayusiyya (to 1074), the Ispahbadiyya (1074 to 1210), centred on Sari and <b>Amol</b>, and the Kinkhwariyya (1238 to 1349).</blockquote><a href="/state/bavand-dynasty">Bavand dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1359 they defeated the Afrasiyabids of Mazandaran, and Qavam al-Din's sons established a theocratic principality based at <b>Amol</b> and Sari.</blockquote><a href="/state/marashi-dynasty">Mar'ashi dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>His descendants ruled this upland between Gilan and <b>Amol</b> as hereditary ostandars.</blockquote><a href="/state/paduspanid-dynasty">Paduspanid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
